6. CONVERSION MECHANISM

6.1 Change Date. Each release or version of the Licensed Work shall include a
    designation of a "Change Date" in the documentation or metadata accompanying such
    release.

6.2 Automatic Conversion. On or after the Change Date, the Licensed Work shall
    automatically convert to the Change License (Apache License 2.0) without further
    action, notice, or requirement for Licensor or You to take any step whatsoever.

6.3 Rights Upon Conversion. Upon conversion to the Change License, You shall have all
    rights and permissions granted by the Apache License 2.0, including the right to
    use the Licensed Work for Commercial Purposes, subject to the terms and conditions
    of the Apache License 2.0.

6.4 No Change Required. No modification, update, or re-release of the Licensed Work is
    required for conversion to take effect. The conversion occurs automatically and
    irrevocably upon the Change Date.

7. INACTIVITY CONVERSION SAFEGUARD

7.1 Automatic Conversion on Inactivity. If no public release of the Licensed Work is
    made for eighteen (18) consecutive calendar months, the most recent publicly
    released version of the Licensed Work shall automatically and irrevocably convert to
    the Change License (Apache License 2.0) without further action or notice.

7.2 Counting of Period. The 18-month period shall be calculated from the date of the
    most recent public release of the Licensed Work. "Public release" means any version
    made available to the general public through official channels, including but not
    limited to GitHub, PyPI, or other public repositories.

7.3 Automatic Effect. This conversion occurs automatically and does not require notice,
    affirmative action, or agreement from any party. All versions released prior to the
    conversion shall remain under this License until the conversion date, at which point
    they shall be governed by the Change License.

8. DERIVATIVE WORKS AND USER COMPONENTS

8.1 Distinction Between Core Modifications and User Components.

    8.1.1 "Core Modifications" means any modification to the Rosh runtime, parser,
          type system, standard library, or any component included in the official
          Rosh distribution. Core Modifications are considered Derivative Works
          subject to this License.

    8.1.2 "User Components" means software components, widgets, libraries, or
          applications created by You that: (a) are designed to work with or extend
          Rosh (using the Rosh Component Contract or documented interfaces);
          (b) are not modifications to the Rosh core; and (c) are distributed
          separately from the Rosh core distribution. User Components are not
          considered Derivative Works for purposes of this License.

8.2 License for Core Modifications.

    Any Core Modification created by You must comply with this License and shall be
    subject to all restrictions, limitations, conversion provisions, and
    non-commercial use requirements herein. Core Modifications remain subject to Section 6
    (Conversion Mechanism) and Section 7 (Inactivity Conversion Safeguard).

8.3 License for User Components.

    8.3.1 Ownership and Control. You retain all ownership rights to User Components
          You create.

    8.3.2 License Choice. You may license User Components under any license of Your
          choosing, including but not limited to:
          (a) Open-source licenses (MIT, GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0, Apache 2.0, BSD, etc.);
          (b) Proprietary or closed-source licenses;
          (c) Dual-license models (free for non-commercial, paid for commercial use);
          (d) Custom or no-license terms (proprietary/all-rights-reserved).

    8.3.3 No Rosh-BSL Requirement. User Components are NOT required to be licensed
          under Rosh-BSL. You are free to apply any license to User Components
          regardless of this License.

    8.3.4 Commercial Rights. User Components may be sold, licensed, sublicensed, or
          monetized independently by You. Licensor has no claim to revenue or
          commercial rights from User Components.

8.4 Component Attribution and Trademark Requirements.

    If You distribute a User Component, the User Component must:

    8.4.1 Attribution. Retain a clear indication (via comments, documentation, or
          metadata) that the User Component is designed for Rosh and includes a
          reference to https://rosh.cloud and/or notice that Rosh is governed by
          Rosh-BSL.

    8.4.2 License Notice. Include a copy of this License or a link to
          https://rosh.cloud/license (for user reference only—not as a requirement
          that User Components themselves be licensed under Rosh-BSL).

    8.4.3 Trademark Protection. You may not use Rosh Trademarks (Section 1.9) in the
          title, branding, or primary identification of Your User Component without
          written permission from Licensor. Acceptable forms include:
          • "[YourName] Widget for Rosh"
          • "[YourName]'s Rosh Component"
          • "[YourName] Plugin for Rosh"
          Unacceptable forms include:
          • "Rosh [YourName]" (as primary branding)
          • "[YourName]'s Rosh" (implies official status)
          • Forked or modified Rosh released under a different name suggesting
            official status

8.5 Official Rosh Library Components.

    Components officially published by Rosh as part of the standard library, bundled
    distribution, or official examples are Core Modifications and remain subject to
    this License, including all restrictions in Section 4. User Components distributed
    separately and independently are not affected by this requirement.

8.6 Modification and Redistribution of Rosh Core.

    If You modify the Rosh core and redistribute such modifications:

    8.6.1 You must comply with all terms of this License, including Section 8.2.

    8.6.2 You may not represent Your modified version as "Rosh" or an official Rosh
          release. You must clearly mark Your modified version as an unofficial fork,
          derivative, or modified version.

    8.6.3 You must retain all copyright notices, license notices, and conversion
          clauses from the original Rosh core.

    8.6.4 You may not remove, disable, obscure, or circumvent the conversion
          mechanism (Section 6 or Section 7) in any modified Rosh core You
          redistribute.

8.7 Distribution of Licensed Work and Core Modifications.

    If You distribute the Rosh core or any Core Modification, You must:

    8.7.1 Include this License in full, including all conversion provisions, with
          the distribution.

    8.7.2 Ensure that recipients are clearly informed they receive the work under
          this License or the Change License (if conversion has occurred).

    8.7.3 Not obscure, remove, or alter any copyright notice, license notice, or
          conversion clause.

8.8 Interoperability of User Components.

    User Components may freely depend upon, call, import, or interoperate with other
    User Components regardless of their respective licenses. This interoperability right
    is not affected by the license You choose for Your User Component or the licenses
    chosen by other component authors. User Components are not required to be compatible
    with each other, but restrictions on interoperability due to license incompatibility
    shall not apply.

9. COMPONENT REGISTRY AND ECOSYSTEM

9.1 Official Rosh Component Registry.

    Licensor maintains or may maintain an official Rosh Component Registry at
    rosh.cloud/components (or such other URL as Licensor designates) listing User
    Components and resources compatible with Rosh.

9.2 User Component Registration.

    You may submit Your User Component to the Rosh Component Registry for listing and
    discovery by the community. Registration does not:
    (a) Transfer ownership or licensing rights to Licensor;
    (b) Require Your User Component to change its license;
    (c) Require Your User Component to comply with Rosh-BSL;
    (d) Grant Licensor any rights to Your User Component beyond listing it in the
        Registry;
    (e) Subject Your User Component to this License.

9.3 Registry Acceptance and Removal.

    9.3.1 Licensor may accept or decline to list any User Component in the Registry
          at its sole discretion, including for reasons of quality, compatibility,
          safety, or community standards.

    9.3.2 Licensor may remove any listed User Component from the Registry if it
          violates applicable law, infringes third-party rights, misrepresents its
          functionality, or fails to remain compatible with current Rosh releases.

    9.3.3 Removal from the Registry does not affect Your rights to use, distribute,
          or license Your User Component independently.

9.4 Registry Listing as Community Benefit.

    Listing in the official Rosh Component Registry serves as a community endorsement
    that Your User Component:
    (a) Is compatible with the declared version(s) of Rosh;
    (b) Follows the Rosh Component Contract (as documented in COMPONENT-CONTRACT.md
        or equivalent);
    (c) Is maintained and available for download.

10. COMMERCIAL LICENSING

10.1 Commercial Use Requires Agreement.

     Any use of the Rosh core (and only the Rosh core) that constitutes Commercial Use
     as defined in Section 1.3 requires a separate written Commercial License Agreement
     with Licensor. Commercial Use of the Rosh core without such an agreement is
     prohibited.

10.2 User Components Not Subject to Commercial Restriction.

     User Components are owned and licensed by their individual authors. User Components
     are not subject to the commercial restrictions of this License and may be used
     commercially by their licensees regardless of whether Licensor has granted a
     Commercial License for the Rosh core.

10.3 Commercial Licensing Process.

     10.3.1 To inquire about a Commercial License for Rosh, contact Licensor at
            licensing@rosh.cloud or visit https://rosh.cloud/licensing.

     10.3.2 Commercial License terms are negotiated individually based on:
            (a) Scope of use (number of users, deployments, or instances);
            (b) Territory and duration;
            (c) Support and maintenance requirements;
            (d) Whether Licensor-provided features or services are required.

     10.3.3 Each Commercial License is a binding written agreement between Licensor and
            the licensee. No implied or oral Commercial Licenses are granted.

10.4 Evaluation Licenses.

     Licensor may grant time-limited, non-commercial evaluation licenses to organisations
     wishing to assess Rosh for commercial use. Evaluation licenses may be granted subject
     to terms including: limited duration (typically 30–90 days), restricted use (testing
     only, not production), and the requirement to purchase a full Commercial License upon
     expiration if You wish to continue commercial use.

